## Design Decisions a Reviewer Will Ask About

**PIT vs no PIT:** If your index is read-mostly (immutable logs, product catalog updated nightly), you can skip PIT and use plain `search_after` on a stable sort — simpler, no cleanup. Use PIT when consistency across pages under live writes matters.

**PIT lifecycle in cursors:** The PIT id lives in the cursor. Downside — a slow client holding an old cursor pins ES resources until keepAlive expires. Keep TTL short (1–2 min per page). For very long sessions, consider stateless `search_after` without PIT and accept eventual-consistency drift.

**Tiebreaker choice:** `_id` works everywhere. For max performance ES recommends the implicit `_shard_doc` tiebreaker (only valid inside a PIT), which avoids loading `_id` field data.

**No total count:** `search_after` deliberately doesn't give you a total. Don't compute it — that defeats the purpose. Use `hasMore` for "next page" UX, not page numbers.

**Cursor tampering:** Base64 is encoding, not security. If cursors must be tamper-proof (e.g. they encode a user-scoped filter), sign them (HMAC) or store server-side and hand out a random token.

